/* Lesson quizzes — batch 4 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p7m1":[
 {id:"p7m1q1", q:"A clinic's imaging server plus a day of lost work is valued at $50,000. A ransomware event would destroy 60% of that value, and you expect one every two years. A $4,000-a-year control would cut the exposure factor to 0.2 and the rate to once a decade. What is the control's net annual value?", options:["$14,000","$8,000","$10,000","$11,000"], answer:2, why:"Before: SLE = 50,000 x 0.6 = $30,000 and ALE = 30,000 x 0.5 = $15,000. After: SLE $10,000, ALE $1,000. Net = 15,000 - 1,000 - 4,000 = $10,000. $14,000 forgets to subtract the control's own annual cost."},
 {id:"p7m1q2", q:"Every Monday the office manager reads the firewall's denied-connection log and opens a ticket for anything unusual. How is this control best classified?", options:["Operational category, detective type","Technical category, detective type","Managerial category, preventive type","Technical category, preventive type"], answer:0, why:"A person carries it out as a routine, so it is operational, and it notices activity after the fact, so it is detective. The firewall rule itself is technical and preventive, but the control described here is the human log review."},
 {id:"p7m1q3", q:"A lost-laptop risk in the register scores likelihood 3 x impact 4 = 12. Which control mainly lowers the impact score rather than the likelihood?", options:["Cable locks and a laptop sign-out sheet","Training staff never to leave laptops in cars","A policy that laptops stay in the office","Full-disk encryption with TPM and remote wipe"], answer:3, why:"Encryption does not make loss less likely; it makes a lost device far less damaging because the data cannot be read, so impact drops. The other three make loss or theft less likely, which moves only the likelihood factor."},
 {id:"p7m1q4", q:"A client's contract promises 99.95% availability for its online booking system. About how much downtime does that allow per year?", options:["About 52.6 minutes","About 4.4 hours","About 8.8 hours","About 26 minutes"], answer:1, why:"Allowed downtime = (1 - 0.9995) x 8,760 h = 0.0005 x 8,760 = 4.38 hours. 8.8 hours is three nines (99.9%) and 52.6 minutes is four nines (99.99%); 99.95% sits between them."},
 {id:"p7m1q5", q:"A design review lists five layers: VPN with MFA, a jump host, the EDR console, the backup console and firewall management. All five log in with the same domain admin account. What is the most important weakness?", options:["Five layers are too many and slow staff down; remove two","The jump host should sit in front of the VPN instead","One stolen login opens every layer; they are not independent","MFA on the VPN is redundant once EDR is deployed everywhere"], answer:2, why:"Defense in depth works only if layers fail independently. One shared admin credential means a single phish or keylogger opens all five, so they act as one layer. Separate accounts with their own MFA restore independence; removing layers would weaken it further."}
],
"p7m2":[
 {id:"p7m2q1", q:"A certificate's SAN list contains example.com and *.example.com. Which hostname will a browser reject with a name mismatch?", options:["www.example.com","portal.eu.example.com","example.com","mail.example.com"], answer:1, why:"A wildcard covers exactly one label level, so *.example.com matches www and mail but not portal.eu, which has two labels before the domain. example.com is listed on its own. The fix is to reissue with portal.eu.example.com as its own SAN."},
 {id:"p7m2q2", q:"Every office PC loads the new intranet portal except one, which reports that the certificate is 'not yet valid'. The chain and SAN list check out from other machines. What is the most likely cause?", options:["The server sends only the leaf, not the intermediate","The certificate is missing that PC's hostname in its SAN list","The portal only supports TLS 1.0","That PC's system clock is set wrong"], answer:3, why:"Validity windows are checked against the client's clock, so one machine set to an earlier date rejects a certificate every other client accepts. Fix NTP. A missing intermediate fails on many clients with an unable-to-verify error, not 'not yet valid'."},
 {id:"p7m2q3", q:"Two of a client's internal services share a secret key. The receiver must confirm each API request was not altered and came from a key holder; non-repudiation is not needed. Which primitive fits best?", options:["An HMAC of the request with the shared key","A plain SHA-256 hash sent with the request","Base64 encoding of the request body","Encrypting the request with the receiver's RSA public key"], answer:0, why:"An HMAC mixes the shared key into the hash, so only key holders can produce a valid tag: integrity plus origin. Anyone who alters a request can simply recompute a plain SHA-256, and RSA encryption gives confidentiality, not proof of origin."},
 {id:"p7m2q4", q:"Code review: records are encrypted with AES-256-GCM, the key lives in a cloud KMS, and the 96-bit nonce is a constant hardcoded in the source. What is the most serious problem?", options:["AES is too weak for records; RSA-4096 should encrypt them","GCM gives no integrity, so a SHA-256 hash must be stored too","A reused nonce under one key breaks GCM's secrecy and integrity","The key should be raised to 512 bits to make up for the fixed nonce"], answer:2, why:"GCM's one hard rule is never to reuse a nonce with the same key; a fixed nonce leaks relationships between plaintexts and lets tags be forged. GCM already authenticates the data, RSA is far too slow for bulk encryption, and AES has no 512-bit key."},
 {id:"p7m2q5", q:"An adversary records a company's TLS 1.3 traffic for a year and then steals the web server's private key. What can they do with the recordings?", options:["Decrypt all of it, because the private key unwraps every recorded session key","Nothing: the ephemeral secrets are gone, so old session keys cannot be rebuilt","Decrypt only the sessions that negotiated AES-128 rather than AES-256","Decrypt every session by recomputing the handshake transcript hashes"], answer:1, why:"TLS 1.3 uses only ephemeral (EC)DHE, and the server's long-term key merely signs the handshake. With the per-session secrets gone, the stolen key cannot rebuild old session keys: forward secrecy. Unwrapping session keys describes RSA key transport, which TLS 1.3 removed."}
],
"p7m3":[
 {id:"p7m3q1", q:"Which has the larger search space: 8 random characters from 94 printable symbols, or a passphrase of 4 random words from a 7,776-word list?", options:["The 8 random characters, by about 1.7 times","The 4-word passphrase, by about 4,600 times","They are equal, because both are chosen at random","The 4-word passphrase, because it contains more characters"], answer:0, why:"94^8 is about 6.1 x 10^15 while 7,776^4 is about 3.7 x 10^15, so the random characters win by roughly 1.7 times. The 4,600 figure is for five words; strength comes from the number of random choices, not the character count."},
 {id:"p7m3q2", q:"A stolen database holds bcrypt hashes (cost 10, unique salts) of passwords that are 7 characters of lowercase letters and digits. At about 10^4 bcrypt guesses per second on one GPU, how long does exhausting one account's keyspace take?", options:["About 8 seconds","About 9 years","About 3 months","About 2 hours"], answer:2, why:"36^7 is about 7.8 x 10^10 candidates; divided by 10^4 per second that is 7.8 x 10^6 seconds, roughly 90 days. Eight seconds is the time at 10^10 SHA-256 guesses per second, which is why fast hashes are wrong for passwords. The salt forces this work per account."},
 {id:"p7m3q3", q:"A user got 30 push-approval prompts at 2 a.m. and approved one to make them stop. The client wants to keep push MFA for now. Which change most directly addresses this failure?", options:["Switch the user to SMS one-time codes sent to their phone","Turn on number matching for all push approvals","Force a password change every 90 days for all staff","Lengthen session lifetimes so fewer prompts are ever sent"], answer:1, why:"This is prompt bombing (MFA fatigue). With number matching, a prompt can only be approved by someone looking at the real login screen, so blind approvals fail. SMS is still phishable and adds SIM-swap risk, and periodic password changes do not stop an attacker who already holds the password."},
 {id:"p7m3q4", q:"A quarterly access review finds that a bookkeeper who moved to the front desk two years ago can still edit the accounting share and run payroll. Which process failed, and what fixes it?", options:["Joiner: she was provisioned with too much access on day one","Leaver: her old account should have been disabled","Authentication: her account needs phishing-resistant MFA","Mover: a role change must also remove the old access"], answer:3, why:"On a role change the mover step adds new access and must remove the old; skipping removal causes privilege creep. Her access was right when she joined, and she has not left. MFA proves who she is but does not limit what she may do."},
 {id:"p7m3q5", q:"A developer builds 'Sign in with Google' by accepting any valid OAuth 2.0 access token as proof of who the user is. What is the core flaw?", options:["An access token delegates API access; identity needs the OIDC ID token","Nothing; OAuth 2.0 on its own is the standard login protocol for SSO","OAuth tokens are never signed, so only SAML assertions can be trusted","Refresh tokens, not access tokens, are what prove the user's identity"], answer:0, why:"OAuth 2.0 is delegated authorization: an access token lets an app call an API on someone's behalf and may not even have been issued to this app. OpenID Connect adds the signed ID token stating who authenticated. Treating OAuth alone as login is the classic mistake."}
],
"p7m4":[
 {id:"p7m4q1", q:"Accounts payable gets an email from a long-time supplier's genuine mailbox asking to switch bank details before Friday's $38,000 payment. SPF, DKIM and DMARC all pass. Which control most reliably prevents the loss?", options:["Reply to the email and ask the supplier to confirm the change","Call the phone number given in the email's signature","Call the supplier back on the number already on file","Trust it, since passing DMARC proves the email is legitimate"], answer:2, why:"If the supplier's real mailbox is compromised, email authentication passes and the attacker reads any reply or plants their own number in the signature. Only an out-of-band callback to a number you already hold breaks the attacker's control of the channel."},
 {id:"p7m4q2", q:"An office has a 500 Mb/s internet link and a firewall rated to inspect 10 Gb/s. A 4 Gb/s volumetric flood is aimed at its public IP. Where must the mitigation happen?", options:["Upstream at the ISP or a CDN, since the flood fills the link eightfold first","At the office firewall, since its 10 Gb/s rating exceeds the 4 Gb/s flood","On the web server, with a host firewall and per-client rate limiting","On the internal switches, with port security on every access port"], answer:0, why:"4 Gb/s arriving at a 0.5 Gb/s link fills the pipe eight times over before a single packet reaches the firewall, so the firewall's rating is irrelevant. Only capacity upstream of the bottleneck, at the ISP or a CDN, can absorb or filter the flood."},
 {id:"p7m4q3", q:"A logged-in customer of a client's portal changes /invoices/1043 to /invoices/1044 in the address bar and sees another customer's invoice. Which OWASP category is this, and what is the fix?", options:["Injection: switch the invoice query to parameterized statements","Authentication failures: require MFA for all customer logins","Cryptographic failures: encrypt invoice numbers so they cannot be guessed","Broken access control: check server-side that the invoice is the user's"], answer:3, why:"The user is properly authenticated but reaches data they are not authorized for, which is A01 Broken Access Control. The fix is a server-side ownership check, deny by default. Obscuring the IDs only hides the hole, and MFA proves identity rather than limiting access."},
 {id:"p7m4q4", q:"At 01:40 EDR alerts that a service account on the file server is deleting volume shadow copies and backup catalogs. No files have been renamed yet. What is the correct first move?", options:["Wait for mass file renames to confirm it is really ransomware","Isolate the host, disable the account, protect the backups","Reboot the server at once to kill the malicious process","Reimage the server from last night's backup immediately"], answer:1, why:"Backup deletion is a late warning that comes before encryption, so act now: isolate, disable the account and protect the backups. Waiting for renames forfeits the last chance to stop it, and rebooting or wiping destroys evidence before the scope is known."},
 {id:"p7m4q5", q:"A client's web app shows visitor comments with element.innerHTML = comment. An authorized assessment reports that a comment containing markup runs script in other visitors' browsers. What is the right fix?", options:["Switch the comment database queries to parameterized statements","Rely on a web application firewall to filter scripts in comments","Limit comments to 200 characters so a script cannot fit","Render comments as text (textContent or encoding) and add a CSP"], answer:3, why:"This is cross-site scripting: untrusted input is being rendered as HTML. Writing it as text (textContent or encoded output) keeps data from becoming code, and a CSP adds a layer. Parameterized queries fix SQL injection, not rendering, and a WAF is only a stopgap."}
],
"p7m5":[
 {id:"p7m5q1", q:"An inter-VLAN firewall has: 1 allow established/related; 2 deny Staff to Server VLAN, any service; 3 allow Staff to 10.10.20.5 on TCP 443. Staff cannot reach the app on 443. Why, and what is the fix?", options:["A stateful firewall needs a separate return rule for replies","Rule 2 matches first and first match wins; move rule 3 above it","The implicit deny at the end overrides any allow rule","Port 443 must also be opened on the Staff VLAN's own interface"], answer:1, why:"Rules are evaluated top to bottom and the first match decides, so the broad deny in rule 2 catches the traffic before rule 3 is ever read. Put the specific allow above the general deny. Stateful firewalls pass replies automatically through rule 1."},
 {id:"p7m5q2", q:"An inline IPS protects the cardholder-data segment of a small payment processor. If the IPS hardware fails, which failure mode should it be configured for?", options:["Fail-open, because availability always outranks security","Fail-open, because an inline device should never stop traffic","Fail-closed, since protecting card data outweighs uptime","Neither; an inline IPS cannot fail, unlike a passive IDS"], answer:2, why:"Fail-open or fail-closed is a deliberate choice per segment. For a cardholder-data zone, letting traffic pass uninspected is worse than a brief outage, so fail-closed fits. A hospital clinical network might choose fail-open because there availability protects patients."},
 {id:"p7m5q3", q:"A client says: 'Our VPN requires MFA, so we are zero trust.' Once connected, any laptop can reach every server on every port. What is the gap?", options:["Nothing; MFA at the VPN gateway is the definition of zero trust","The VPN needs a longer pre-shared key to count as zero trust","Zero trust only applies to cloud apps, not on-premises servers","The VPN trusts the device once inside; zero trust checks every request"], answer:3, why:"A traditional VPN authenticates at connection time and then places the device on the network with broad reach. Zero trust evaluates every request to each resource using identity, device health and least privilege, for example via ZTNA per application, so location grants nothing."},
 {id:"p7m5q4", q:"An office's IP cameras must talk only to the video recorder and nothing else. Which design achieves that?", options:["A camera VLAN allowing only RTSP 554 to the recorder, denying all else","A camera VLAN routed to the staff VLAN by an any-any rule for easy viewing","Cameras left on the staff VLAN but given strong, unique admin passwords","Cameras placed in the DMZ so the vendor can manage them from the internet"], answer:0, why:"Isolation comes from the firewall rules between VLANs, not the VLAN tag itself. Allow exactly one flow to the recorder, deny the rest and block egress, since cameras never need the internet. An any-any rule erases the separation, and passwords do not limit reach."},
 {id:"p7m5q5", q:"You added an allow rule for Staff to the file server on TCP 445. From a staff PC, nc -vz 10.10.20.5 445 still fails, and the firewall's deny log (every deny rule logs) shows nothing for that flow. Where should you look next?", options:["A broader deny rule sitting above the new allow rule","The missing return rule that the stateful firewall needs","The server: its host firewall, and whether ss -tlnp shows a listener","The rule's review date, which must be set before it takes effect"], answer:2, why:"If the network firewall had dropped the traffic, the logged deny would show the 5-tuple. With no deny recorded, the block is beyond it: a host firewall on the destination or a service not listening, which ss -tlnp on the server confirms."}
],
"p7m6":[
 {id:"p7m6q1", q:"A client produces 2,000,000 log events a day, 20 of them malicious. A detector catches 95% of malicious events and fires on 0.05% of benign ones. About what share of its alerts are real?", options:["About 95%","About 50%","About 99.95%","About 2%"], answer:3, why:"True alerts: 0.95 x 20 = 19. False alerts: 0.0005 x 1,999,980, about 1,000. Precision = 19 / 1,019, about 1.9%. 95% is the detection rate (recall), not the share of alerts that are real; the huge benign base rate swamps it."},
 {id:"p7m6q2", q:"You are sizing log storage for a client: 120 endpoints, about 15,000 events per endpoint per day, about 400 bytes per event, kept for the CIS minimum of 90 days. Roughly how much storage is that before compression?", options:["About 6.5 GB","About 65 GB","About 650 GB","About 263 GB"], answer:1, why:"120 x 15,000 x 400 bytes = 720 MB a day, and 90 x 720 MB = 64.8 GB. 263 GB would be a full year of retention, while 6.5 or 650 GB come from slipping a factor of ten somewhere."},
 {id:"p7m6q3", q:"A compromised server is still running and the case may go to law enforcement. In what order should evidence be collected?", options:["Memory, then live connections and processes, then disk, then logs","A disk image, then memory, then logs, then network connections","Logs and backups first, then a disk image, then memory last","Power off to freeze the state, then image the disk, then pull logs"], answer:0, why:"RFC 3227's order of volatility collects what disappears first: memory and live connections vanish at shutdown, while disks and logs persist. Powering off destroys memory evidence, and imaging the disk first lets the volatile state change. Hash every image and record custody."},
 {id:"p7m6q4", q:"A rule alerts on an encoded PowerShell command on the IT admin's workstation. Investigation shows the admin ran an approved maintenance script inside a scheduled change window. How should the alert be classified?", options:["False positive","False negative","Benign true positive","True negative"], answer:2, why:"The detection caught real activity that turned out to be authorized, which the lesson calls a benign true positive. Labelling it a false positive hides that the rule worked; the right follow-up is to scope it, for example to change windows, not to delete it."},
 {id:"p7m6q5", q:"A threat report on a data-theft crew lists file hashes, IP addresses, sender domains, and one behavior: servers copying large volumes to cloud storage with a sync tool at night. Which detection will stay useful longest?", options:["Block every listed IP address at the perimeter firewall","Alert on servers bulk-uploading to cloud storage after hours","Alert in EDR whenever one of the listed file hashes appears","Block the campaign's current sender domains in the mail filter"], answer:1, why:"Hashes, IPs and domains cost the attacker almost nothing to rotate, so they age within days. A detection on the behavior survives new infrastructure, which is the 'pyramid of pain'. Adding the indicators is still fine, but the behavioral rule is the durable one."}
],
"p7m7":[
 {id:"p7m7q1", q:"Leadership's document says 'all remote access requires MFA.' A new document states: 'MFA means FIDO2 security keys for administrators and number-matched push or stronger for staff.' What type of document is the new one?", options:["Policy","Standard","Procedure","Guideline"], answer:1, why:"A policy states intent; a standard makes it measurable and mandatory by naming exactly which methods count. A procedure would give the step-by-step enrollment, and a guideline would only recommend, not require."},
 {id:"p7m7q2", q:"A client's office manager emails you: 'Go ahead and run a vulnerability scan on our office network this weekend.' The owner has not been involved. What do you need before scanning?", options:["Nothing more; her email is written and she works there","Nothing, because Van Buren and the 2022 DOJ policy protect good-faith scanning","A verbal okay from the owner by phone, noted in the ticket","Written authorization from the owner, with assets, ranges and time window"], answer:3, why:"Authorization must come in writing from someone with authority over the systems, with an explicit scope. An employee's request is not the owner's consent, and Van Buren and the DOJ charging policy are not a licence: civil suits and state law such as Texas Penal Code 33.02 still apply."},
 {id:"p7m7q3", q:"On March 3, a Texas landscaping company determines that an unencrypted spreadsheet with the Social Security numbers of 400 Texans was stolen. Under Texas law, what are its notification deadlines?", options:["Individuals by May 2 and the attorney general by April 2","The state regulator within 72 hours; individuals are optional","Individuals by April 2 and the attorney general by May 2","None, because fewer than 500 people are affected"], answer:0, why:"Texas requires notice to individuals within 60 days of determining the breach (May 2) and to the attorney general within 30 days when 250 or more Texans are affected (April 2). 72 hours is GDPR's regulator deadline, and 500 is the FTC Safeguards Rule threshold."},
 {id:"p7m7q4", q:"Phishing simulations at a 60-person client. Month 1: 9 clicked (15%), 6 reported (10%). Month 3: 6 clicked (10%), 24 reported (40%), first report after 7 minutes. What is the best reading?", options:["Failing: six people still clicked, so the click rate must reach zero","No conclusion is possible until clicks are tracked alone for a year","Working: reports outnumber clicks and arrive within ten minutes","Worse: four times as many people now interact with the phish"], answer:2, why:"The report rate is the number that saves you, because one early report lets you purge every copy before others click. Reports rising past clicks with a 7-minute first report meets the lesson's targets; demanding zero clicks and shaming clickers misreads the goal."},
 {id:"p7m7q5", q:"A 35-employee Texas accounting firm wants SB 2610's protection from exemplary damages if it is breached. What must it have had in place at the time?", options:["Written password policies and staff training only","A full framework such as ISO 27001 or NIST CSF","A current SOC 2 Type II report from an outside auditor","A documented program conforming to CIS Controls IG1"], answer:3, why:"SB 2610 scales by size: under 20 employees, password policies and training; 20 to 99, CIS Controls Implementation Group 1; 100 to 249, a full framework. At 35 staff the firm needs a documented IG1 program; the password-and-training tier applies only below 20."}
],
"p7m8":[
 {id:"p7m8q1", q:"An uncredentialed external scan of a client's public IP finds only port 443 open and reports no missing patches. Yet the RMM shows their laptops have not installed OS updates in four months. What explains the clean report?", options:["An uncredentialed external scan cannot see patches inside the OS","The laptops must actually be patched, or the scanner would have found the gaps","External scans read installed versions directly, so the RMM data must be wrong","Laptops behind NAT have no vulnerabilities that are worth reporting"], answer:0, why:"A stranger's view from the internet shows only what listens on public IPs. Missing OS patches on laptops behind NAT are invisible to it; a credentialed internal scan or an endpoint agent reads installed versions. A clean uncredentialed scan is not evidence of patching."},
 {id:"p7m8q2", q:"A credentialed scan flags an Ubuntu server's OpenSSH as critical because its version string is older than the upstream fixed release. Ubuntu's security notice lists the installed package build as containing the fix. What do you do?", options:["Build the latest upstream OpenSSH from source tonight","Disable SSH on the server until a newer version string appears","Record a validated false positive, citing the backport advisory","Raise it to P1 because the scanner rated it critical"], answer:2, why:"Distributions often backport security fixes without changing the upstream version string, so version-based checks misfire. Confirm against the vendor's advisory, then record a validated false positive. Rebuilding from source overnight takes the server off the vendor's patch stream for no gain."},
 {id:"p7m8q3", q:"Using the lesson's patch SLA policy, which tier fits a CVSS 6.5 flaw in a remote-support tool installed on 12 laptops that CISA has just added to the KEV catalog?", options:["P3, within 60-90 days, because it is only medium severity","P2, within 14-30 days, because the laptops are internal","P4, at the next scheduled maintenance window","P1, within 72 hours to 7 days, because it is on the KEV catalog"], answer:3, why:"The policy puts anything on the KEV catalog in P1 regardless of CVSS, because confirmed exploitation is a likelihood signal that severity scores ignore. Sorting by the 6.5 'medium' score alone is the severity-versus-risk trap the lesson warns about."},
 {id:"p7m8q4", q:"Three days after Patch Tuesday the patch tool reports a browser update 'installed' on all 40 PCs, but a rescan still flags 14 of them. What is the most likely explanation and next step?", options:["The rescan is wrong; close the tickets because the tool says installed","A pending restart or an old copy left on disk; fix it, then rescan","The scanner's browser checks are unreliable and should be switched off","The 14 PCs should be reimaged from the standard image right away"], answer:1, why:"'Installed' with a pending restart, or an old version left on disk, is not 'fixed'; verification is the rescan, not the patch tool's word. Trusting the tool leaves real exposure open, and reimaging 14 machines is a costly answer to what is usually a restart."},
 {id:"p7m8q5", q:"A 12-person client has EDR on every endpoint. At 2:13 a.m. on a Saturday it raises a high-severity alert on the file server, and nobody sees it until Monday. What is the real gap?", options:["No one watches and responds around the clock; EDR needs MDR or an on-call responder","The EDR product is too weak and should be replaced with next-generation antivirus","EDR should be upgraded to XDR, which removes the need for human analysts","More folder exclusions are needed so EDR raises fewer alerts overall"], answer:0, why:"EDR records and can block, but an alert nobody reads at 2 a.m. is only a recorder. MDR, a SOC watching and acting 24x7, closes the gap. XDR correlates more signals but still needs people, and exclusions create blind spots where malware hides."}
],
"p7m9":[
 {id:"p7m9q1", q:"A 3 TB file server must be back within 12 hours (RTO). Restore paths: the cloud copy over a 200 Mb/s link, or the local appliance over 1 Gb/s at about 70% efficiency. Which meets the RTO?", options:["Both; the cloud restore takes only about 4.2 hours","Neither; even the local restore takes more than a day","Only the cloud copy, since object storage is faster than local disks","Only the local restore, at about 9.5 hours; the cloud takes about 33 hours"], answer:3, why:"3 TB is 2.4 x 10^13 bits. At 200 Mb/s that is 120,000 s, about 33 hours; at 700 Mb/s effective it is about 34,000 s, about 9.5 hours. The 4.2-hour figure forgets to convert bytes to bits. Keep the cloud copy for site disasters."},
 {id:"p7m9q2", q:"A BIA says the order system may lose at most 2 hours of data (RPO) and must run again within 6 hours (RTO). Backups run nightly at 23:00, and a test restore takes 3 hours. What must change?", options:["Back up at least every 2 hours; the restore time is fine","Nothing, because the 3-hour restore beats the 6-hour RTO","Buy a faster link so that restores finish within 2 hours","Add RAID so that no data is lost between nightly backups"], answer:0, why:"RPO sets backup frequency: with nightly jobs, a failure at 22:00 loses almost a day of orders. RTO sets restore speed, which already fits. Meeting the RTO says nothing about the RPO, and RAID copies deletion or encryption instantly, so it is not a backup."},
 {id:"p7m9q3", q:"During an incident you learn the attackers were in the network for 21 days before encrypting. Backups keep 14 daily restore points and nothing older. What is the problem?", options:["None, because 14 restore points satisfies the 3-2-1 rule","Too few copies; adding a RAID mirror would fix it","Every restore point may already hold the attackers' tools","The nightly jobs should be differentials rather than incrementals"], answer:2, why:"Retention must outlast dwell time. With 21 days of presence and only 14 days of history, there may be no restore point that predates the intrusion. Keep about 30 dailies plus monthlies and know which point is clean; more copies of the same window do not help."},
 {id:"p7m9q4", q:"Full backup Sunday night, then a backup each night Monday to Thursday. The server dies Friday morning. How many backup sets does the restore need if the nightly jobs were incrementals, and if they were differentials?", options:["Incremental 2, differential 5","Incremental 5, differential 5","Incremental 1, differential 2","Incremental 5, differential 2"], answer:3, why:"An incremental holds changes since the last backup of any kind, so you need the full plus all four nightly sets: 5. A differential holds changes since the last full, so the full plus Thursday's differential is enough: 2. Swapping them reverses the trade-off."},
 {id:"p7m9q5", q:"Your MSP's remote-management tool can run scripts on all 22 client networks. Which control set most directly limits the damage if one technician's account is phished?", options:["A 90-day password rotation for every technician account","Phishing-resistant MFA, per-client least privilege and mass-action alerts","A web application firewall placed in front of the RMM console","One shared technician login so every action comes from a single audited account"], answer:1, why:"Phishing-resistant MFA stops the stolen credential working, least privilege per technician and client shrinks what any one account reaches, and mass-action alerts catch abuse. A shared login removes accountability and widens access, and password rotation does not stop a real-time phish."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p9m1":[
 {id:"p9m1q1", q:"Five past jobs placed 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 km of cable and took 3, 4, 6, 7 and 10 hours. Fitting y = w*x + b by least squares, what are w and b?", options:["w = 2.0, b = 0.0","w = 1.7, b = 0.9","w = 1.75, b = 1.25","w = 1.7, b = 6.0"], answer:1, why:"With means 3 and 6, the sum of (x - 3)(y - 6) is 17 and the sum of (x - 3)^2 is 10, so w = 1.7 and b = 6 - 1.7 x 3 = 0.9. The pair 1.75 and 1.25 is the line through the two endpoints, which ignores the middle points."},
 {id:"p9m1q2", q:"A model forecasting next week's call volume was evaluated by randomly shuffling three years of daily data into train and test sets. Test error was excellent, but in production it is poor. What is the most likely cause and fix?", options:["Underfitting; the model needs more layers and longer training","The test set was too large; cut it to 5% of the data","Too few epochs; train until the training loss reaches zero","Shuffling leaked future days into training; split by time instead"], answer:3, why:"A random shuffle puts days that come after the test days into training, so the model effectively saw the future and the test score was inflated. Training on earlier data and testing on later data matches real use. A bigger model would only memorize more."},
 {id:"p9m1q3", q:"A model's training MSE is 0.31 and its validation MSE is 0.33, while the data's noise floor is about 0.04. What is the diagnosis and the right direction?", options:["Underfitting (high bias): use a richer model or less regularization","Overfitting (high variance): add regularization and simplify the model","Leakage: hunt for features that are only known after the outcome","Tuned to the validation set: open the test set now to check"], answer:0, why:"High training error with a similar validation error, both far above the noise floor, means the model family cannot capture the pattern: high bias. More regularization or a simpler model would make it worse. Overfitting looks the opposite way: low training error, much higher validation error."},
 {id:"p9m1q4", q:"You run 10-fold cross-validation on 5,000 examples. Which description is correct?", options:["Each run trains on 500 and validates on 4,500; each example is validated 10 times","Each run trains on 4,000 and validates on 1,000; there are 5 trainings in total","Each run trains on 4,500 and validates on 500; each example is validated once, over 10 trainings","Each run trains on 4,500 and validates on 500; one trained model is reused for every fold"], answer:2, why:"Ten folds of 500: each run holds out one fold and trains on the other nine (4,500), so every example is validated exactly once and the price is ten trainings. Averaging the ten scores is far steadier than a single split on small data."},
 {id:"p9m1q5", q:"A team tries 200 hyperparameter settings, scores each on the test set, and reports the best test score as its expected real-world accuracy. Why is that number untrustworthy?", options:["It is trustworthy, because the model never trained on the test data","The 200 settings overfit the training set, not the test set","The test set should have been larger than the training set","Picking the best of 200 on the test set makes that score optimistic"], answer:3, why:"Each comparison against the test set nudges choices toward that particular sample, and taking the best of 200 noisy scores selects luck as well as quality. Tune on a validation set or with cross-validation and open the test set once; merely not training on it is not enough."}
],
"p9m2":[
 {id:"p9m2q1", q:"A neuron has inputs x = (2, -1, 0.5), weights w = (0.3, 0.8, -0.4) and bias 0.2. What does it output through ReLU, and through a sigmoid?", options:["ReLU 0, sigmoid about 0.450","ReLU -0.2, sigmoid about 0.450","ReLU 0, sigmoid about 0.550","ReLU 0.2, sigmoid about 0.550"], answer:0, why:"z = 0.6 - 0.8 - 0.2 + 0.2 = -0.2. ReLU outputs max(0, z) = 0, never a negative number. The sigmoid gives 1/(1 + e^0.2), about 0.450; 0.550 comes from dropping the minus sign and computing sigmoid(+0.2)."},
 {id:"p9m2q2", q:"How many parameters does a fully connected network with layer sizes 1,000 -> 256 -> 64 -> 5 have?", options:["272,704","1,325","273,029","256,256"], answer:2, why:"Each layer has inputs x outputs weights plus one bias per output: 256,256 + 16,448 + 325 = 273,029. 272,704 forgets the 325 biases, and 1,325 just adds up the layer widths. In 32-bit floats the model takes about 1.1 MB."},
 {id:"p9m2q3", q:"Naive softmax code returns NaN for the logits (1003, 1001, 1001). What should a correct implementation return, and how?", options:["About 0.334, 0.333, 0.333, by dividing each logit by their sum","About 0.787, 0.107, 0.107, by first subtracting the largest logit","NaN, because logits that large cannot be turned into probabilities","1, 0, 0, because the largest logit always takes all of the probability"], answer:1, why:"Softmax is unchanged by subtracting a constant, so compute it on (0, -2, -2): e^0 = 1 and e^-2 = 0.135, sum 1.271, giving 0.787, 0.107, 0.107. Dividing logits by their sum is not softmax, and the NaN comes only from evaluating e^1003 directly."},
 {id:"p9m2q4", q:"A batch X of 32 examples with 784 features enters a layer of 128 neurons whose W has shape 128 x 784. The layer computes H = f(X W^T + b). What is H's shape, and what happens if you write X @ W instead?", options:["128 x 32; X @ W gives the same result, just transposed","32 x 784; a layer keeps its input width, so X @ W works too","784 x 128; the batch dimension is absorbed; X @ W also works","32 x 128; X @ W fails, since 784 and 128 do not match"], answer:3, why:"(32 x 784) times W^T (784 x 128) gives 32 x 128: one row per example, one column per neuron. X @ W pairs 784 with 128, which do not match, producing PyTorch's 'mat1 and mat2 shapes cannot be multiplied' error. The batch stays the first axis."},
 {id:"p9m2q5", q:"An 8-layer network uses sigmoid in every hidden layer, and its earliest layers barely change during training. At most, how much is a gradient scaled passing back through those 8 sigmoids, and what is the usual fix?", options:["At most about 1.5 x 10^-5; switch the hidden layers to ReLU","At most 2.0, the sum 8 x 0.25; add more sigmoid layers","At most 0.25, since depth does not compound the effect","Exactly 1, so the learning rate must be the problem"], answer:0, why:"The sigmoid's slope is at most 0.25 and the factors multiply: 0.25^8 is about 1.5 x 10^-5, so early layers receive almost no signal. ReLU's slope is exactly 1 wherever it is active. Adding the factors instead of multiplying them is the tempting error."}
],
"p9m3":[
 {id:"p9m3q1", q:"Two training points: x = (1, 2), y = (3, 5). The model y-hat = w*x + b uses MSE loss, starting at w = b = 0 with learning rate 0.1. With dL/dw = (2/n) sum((y-hat - y) x) and dL/db = (2/n) sum(y-hat - y), what are w and b after one step?", options:["w = -1.3, b = -0.8","w = 13, b = 8","w = 1.3, b = 0.8","w = 0.65, b = 0.4"], answer:2, why:"The errors are -3 and -5, so dL/dw = (2/2)(-3 x 1 - 5 x 2) = -13 and dL/db = -8. The update subtracts the learning rate times the gradient: w = 0 - 0.1 x (-13) = 1.3 and b = 0.8. Negative values come from stepping uphill, along the gradient."},
 {id:"p9m3q2", q:"Two classifiers give the correct class these probabilities on the same three examples. A: 0.6, 0.6, 0.6. B: 0.99, 0.99, 0.05. Which has the lower mean cross-entropy loss?", options:["A, about 0.51 versus about 1.01 for B","B, because its mean probability for the correct class (0.68) beats A's (0.60)","B, because it is right with higher confidence on two of the three","They tie, because cross-entropy depends only on which examples are right"], answer:0, why:"Mean cross-entropy averages -ln p. A costs -ln 0.6 = 0.51 on each example. B costs 0.01 + 0.01 + 3.00 (from -ln 0.05), about 1.01 on average. One confident wrong answer outweighs many mild right ones, which is exactly the pressure training needs."},
 {id:"p9m3q3", q:"Network: z1 = w1*x + b1, h = ReLU(z1), z2 = w2*h + b2, p = sigmoid(z2), loss L = -ln p for target 1. With x = 2, w1 = 0.5, b1 = 0, w2 = 0.4, b2 = -0.4, what is dL/dw1?", options:["-1.0","0, because ReLU blocks the gradient","-0.2","-0.4"], answer:3, why:"Forward: z1 = 1, h = 1, z2 = 0, p = 0.5. Backward: dL/dz2 = p - 1 = -0.5; dL/dh = -0.5 x w2 = -0.2; ReLU passes it because z1 > 0; dL/dw1 = -0.2 x x = -0.4. -1.0 skips the factor w2, and -0.2 forgets to multiply by the input."},
 {id:"p9m3q4", q:"Before a long run, you train a new model on one fixed batch of 32 examples for 500 steps, and the loss stays far from zero. What does this most likely mean?", options:["Overfitting; add dropout and weight decay before the full run","A bug, such as misaligned labels; fix it before scaling up","Too little data; collect more examples before debugging","Nothing; a 32-example batch is simply too noisy to fit"], answer:1, why:"A correct model with enough capacity can memorize a tiny batch almost perfectly, so failing to points to a bug: labels shuffled against inputs, weights not updating or a broken loss. Overfitting is the opposite problem, and more data cannot fix broken code."},
 {id:"p9m3q5", q:"You plan to train a 1-billion-parameter model on 20 billion tokens (the Chinchilla ratio). Each GPU sustains 200 TFLOP/s. Using C = 6ND, roughly how many GPU-hours does the run need?", options:["About 56 GPU-hours","About 10,000 GPU-hours","About 167 GPU-hours","About 600,000 GPU-hours"], answer:2, why:"C = 6 x 10^9 x 2 x 10^10 = 1.2 x 10^20 FLOPs. Divided by 2 x 10^14 FLOP/s that is 6 x 10^5 GPU-seconds, about 167 GPU-hours. 56 hours uses the forward-only 2 FLOPs per parameter per token, and 600,000 forgets to convert seconds to hours."}
],
"p9m4":[
 {id:"p9m4q1", q:"A convolution layer takes a 32 x 32 input with 32 channels and applies 64 filters of size 5 x 5, stride 1, no padding. What is the output's spatial size, and how many parameters does the layer have?", options:["32 x 32; 51,264 parameters","28 x 28; 1,664 parameters","27 x 27; 51,200 parameters","28 x 28; 51,264 parameters"], answer:3, why:"Output width = (32 - 5 + 0)/1 + 1 = 28. Each filter spans all 32 input channels: 5 x 5 x 32 x 64 = 51,200 weights plus 64 biases = 51,264. 1,664 forgets the input channels, and keeping 32 x 32 would need padding of 2."},
 {id:"p9m4q2", q:"One query q = (1, 1, 0, 0) attends over two keys, k1 = (1, 1, 0, 0) and k2 = (0, 0, 1, 1), whose values are v1 = (2, 0) and v2 = (0, 4); d_k = 4. What does scaled dot-product attention output?", options:["About (1.76, 0.48)","About (1.46, 1.08)","(1.0, 2.0)","(2.0, 0.0)"], answer:1, why:"The scores are 2 and 0; dividing by the square root of 4 gives 1 and 0, and softmax gives weights 0.731 and 0.269. Output = 0.731 x (2, 0) + 0.269 x (0, 4) = (1.46, 1.08). (1.76, 0.48) skips the scaling, and (2, 0) is a hard argmax."},
 {id:"p9m4q3", q:"A student's GPT-style model reaches near-zero training loss within an hour but generates nonsense. The code computes attention over the full sequence during training with no causal mask. What went wrong?", options:["Each position saw its own next token, so it learned to copy","The model needs more attention heads to capture syntax","Without a mask the softmax overflows and produces NaN","Position embeddings are missing, so word order is lost"], answer:0, why:"The causal mask sets scores for later positions to minus infinity so each position sees only the past. Without it the answer is visible in training and the loss collapses; at generation time the future does not exist, so the copying trick fails. Missing positions would not drive loss to zero."},
 {id:"p9m4q4", q:"Using about 12d^2 parameters per decoder block, estimate the size of a model with d = 1,024, 24 blocks and a 50,257-token vocabulary (ignore position embeddings and norms).", options:["About 101 million","About 302 million","About 354 million","About 403 million"], answer:2, why:"Blocks: 12 x 1,024^2 x 24 = 302 million (4d^2 for attention plus 8d^2 for the MLP in each). The embedding table adds 50,257 x 1,024 = 51.5 million, about 354 million in total. 302 million forgets the embeddings, and 101 million counts only the attention matrices."},
 {id:"p9m4q5", q:"A chat prompt grows from 4,000 to 32,000 tokens. By roughly what factor does the number of attention scores per head per layer grow during prefill?", options:["8 times","64 times","512 times","About the same, since FlashAttention removed the quadratic cost"], answer:1, why:"Scores form an n x n matrix, so 8 times the tokens gives 8^2 = 64 times the scores. FlashAttention computes the same scores in tiles kept in fast on-chip memory; it avoids storing the full matrix but does not remove the quadratic amount of work."}
],
"p9m5":[
 {id:"p9m5q1", q:"A model's average next-token loss on held-out text is 1.5 nats. What is its perplexity, and what does that mean?", options:["1.5: it is choosing among about one and a half tokens","About 2.25: the loss squared, the number of likely tokens","About 4.5: like choosing uniformly among four or five tokens","About 31.6: ten raised to the loss, the effective vocabulary"], answer:2, why:"Perplexity is e raised to the average loss in nats: e^1.5 is about 4.48, read as an effective number of equally likely choices. Ten to the power would apply only if the loss were measured in base-10 logarithms, which LLM losses are not."},
 {id:"p9m5q2", q:"The next-token logits are A = 3.0 and B = 1.0. What probability does A get at temperature 2?", options:["About 0.73","About 0.88","About 0.98","Exactly 0.50"], answer:0, why:"Temperature divides the logits, giving 1.5 and 0.5, so p(A) = 1/(1 + e^-1), about 0.731. 0.88 is the T = 1 value, and 0.98 comes from multiplying by T instead of dividing. Higher temperature flattens the distribution but does not make it uniform."},
 {id:"p9m5q3", q:"Next-token probabilities, sorted: 0.50, 0.25, 0.12, 0.08, 0.05. With top-p = 0.8, which tokens remain, and what is the top token's probability after renormalizing?", options:["The top two; about 0.67","All five; still 0.50","The top three; still 0.50","The top three; about 0.57"], answer:3, why:"Top-p keeps the smallest set whose probabilities reach at least 0.8: 0.50 + 0.25 = 0.75 falls short, and adding 0.12 gives 0.87. Renormalized, the top token is 0.50 / 0.87, about 0.575. Stopping at two leaves the kept mass below p."},
 {id:"p9m5q4", q:"A company's assistant invents SKUs and prices for its 2026 product catalog. A teammate proposes another round of RLHF so the model 'learns the catalog'. What is the better plan?", options:["Run RLHF anyway; preference tuning is how models gain new facts","Retrieve catalog entries into the prompt and require citations","Set temperature to 0 so the model can only produce correct prices","Enlarge the context window so the model remembers older catalogs"], answer:1, why:"RLHF shapes style, helpfulness and refusals; knowledge comes from pretraining or from documents supplied at inference. Grounding answers in the retrieved catalog, with checkable citations and permission to abstain, attacks the cause. Temperature 0 makes output deterministic, not correct."},
 {id:"p9m5q5", q:"With a 20,000-token prompt, a local model takes 3 s to the first token and then generates 40 tokens per second. You swap in a GPU with twice the TFLOPS but the same memory bandwidth. What mainly changes?", options:["Decode speed roughly doubles, since generation scales with TFLOPS","Both phases speed up exactly twofold","Time to first token drops; decode speed barely changes","Nothing, because inference does not depend on the GPU"], answer:2, why:"Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel and is compute-bound, so more TFLOPS shortens the time to first token. Decode reads every weight for each new token and is memory-bandwidth-bound, so with equal bandwidth tokens per second stays about the same."}
],
"p9m6":[
 {id:"p9m6q1", q:"How many FLOPs does multiplying a 2,048 x 8,192 matrix by an 8,192 x 1,024 matrix take?", options:["About 34 GFLOP","About 17 GFLOP","About 2.1 million FLOPs","About 137 GFLOP"], answer:0, why:"The output has m x n = 2,048 x 1,024 entries, each a dot product of length k = 8,192, so 2mnk = 2 x 2,048 x 1,024 x 8,192, about 3.4 x 10^10 FLOPs. 17 GFLOP counts each multiply-add as one FLOP, and 2.1 million counts only the outputs."},
 {id:"p9m6q2", q:"A CUDA kernel processes n = 5,000,000 elements with 512 threads per block, each thread handling one element behind an if (i < n) guard. How many blocks do you launch, and what does the guard do?", options:["9,765 blocks; the leftover elements are simply skipped","5,000,000 blocks; each block runs a single thread","9,766 blocks; the guard is optional because extra threads write harmlessly","9,766 blocks; the guard idles the 192 surplus threads in the last block"], answer:3, why:"Round up: 5,000,000 / 512 = 9,765.6, so 9,766 blocks launching 5,000,192 threads. The guard stops the 192 extra threads from touching memory past the arrays. Rounding down to 9,765 would leave 320 elements unprocessed, and unguarded writes corrupt memory."},
 {id:"p9m6q3", q:"A GPU peaks at 200 TFLOP/s with 1 TB/s of memory bandwidth. A kernel computes y = a*x + y on FP32 vectors: 2 FLOPs per element while reading x and y and writing y (12 bytes). By the roofline, what limits it and what can it attain?", options:["Compute-bound; close to 200 TFLOP/s","Memory-bound; about 0.17 TFLOP/s","Memory-bound; about 200 TFLOP/s once the data is cached","Compute-bound; about 33 TFLOP/s, a sixth of peak"], answer:1, why:"Intensity is 2 / 12, about 0.17 FLOP per byte, far below the ridge point of 200 / 1 = 200 FLOP per byte. Attainable = min(200 TFLOP/s, 0.17 x 1 TB/s), about 0.17 TFLOP/s. Each value is used once, so caching cannot rescue it."},
 {id:"p9m6q4", q:"You want to fully fine-tune a 3B-parameter model with mixed precision and Adam on a 24 GB laptop GPU. Will it fit?", options:["No: the training state alone is about 48 GB; use LoRA or QLoRA","Yes: the BF16 weights are only 6 GB, leaving plenty of room","Yes, as long as everything is switched to FP32 for stability","No, but only because of activations; weights and Adam state need 12 GB"], answer:0, why:"Mixed-precision Adam carries about 16 bytes per parameter (BF16 weight and gradient, FP32 master copy, two FP32 moments): 3 x 10^9 x 16 = 48 GB before activations. That the 6 GB of weights fit says nothing about training; LoRA or QLoRA train small adapters on a frozen base."},
 {id:"p9m6q5", q:"During training, nvidia-smi shows GPU utilization stuck near 30% with VRAM half used, while one CPU core sits at 100%. What is the most likely cause and first fix?", options:["The GPU is too small; move the whole job to a larger card","Thermal throttling; switch the laptop to a performance profile","The data loader is starving the GPU; add workers and pinned memory","The model is memory-bound; quantize the weights down to 4 bits"], answer:2, why:"Low utilization with spare VRAM and a saturated CPU core means the GPU is waiting for batches. More DataLoader workers, pinned memory and pre-tokenized data on fast storage feed it faster. A bigger GPU would wait just the same, and throttling shows as falling clocks and power, not a busy CPU."}
],
"p9m7":[
 {id:"p9m7q1", q:"At $3 per million input tokens and $15 per million output tokens, a feature makes 20,000 calls a day, each with a 3,000-token prompt and a 400-token answer. About what does it cost per 30-day month?", options:["$300","$27,720","$5,400","$9,000"], answer:3, why:"Per call: 3,000 x $3/10^6 + 400 x $15/10^6 = $0.009 + $0.006 = $0.015. Times 20,000 calls is $300 a day, about $9,000 a month. $5,400 ignores the output tokens, and $27,720 swaps the two prices."},
 {id:"p9m7q2", q:"A query embedding is q = (1, 2, 2). Chunk A is (2, 1, 2) and chunk D is (4, 4, 0). Which chunk is more similar by cosine similarity?", options:["A: about 0.89, versus about 0.71 for D","D: its dot product with q, 12, beats A's 8","D: longer vectors carry more meaning, so it ranks higher","They tie, because both dot products are positive"], answer:0, why:"Cosine divides the dot product by both lengths. |q| = 3 and |A| = 3, so A scores 8/9, about 0.89; |D| is about 5.66, so D scores 12/16.97, about 0.71. The raw dot product rewards D's length, not its direction."},
 {id:"p9m7q3", q:"You index a manual of about 400,000 tokens in 500-token chunks that overlap by 100 tokens, using 768-dimensional float32 embeddings. Roughly how many chunks are there, and how big is the vector index?", options:["About 800 chunks and about 2.4 MB","About 4,000 chunks and about 12 MB","About 1,000 chunks and about 3 MB","About 1,000 chunks and about 768 KB"], answer:2, why:"With 100 tokens of overlap the stride is 400, so 400,000 / 400 gives about 1,000 chunks. Each vector is 768 x 4 bytes = 3 KB, about 3 MB in all. 800 ignores the overlap, and 768 KB forgets that each number takes 4 bytes."},
 {id:"p9m7q4", q:"A company RAG bot answers 'How much vacation do I earn?' wrongly. Logs show the retriever, a keyword (TF-IDF) index, returned five chunks and none was the paid-time-off policy. What is the right fix?", options:["Make the generation prompt more forceful about accuracy","Switch to dense or hybrid retrieval and measure recall@k","Fine-tune the model on the whole handbook so it remembers the policy","Raise the temperature so the model explores more possible answers"], answer:1, why:"This is a retrieval failure: 'vacation' shares no words with 'paid time off', so lexical search cannot find it. Dense embeddings place the phrases close together, and recall@k shows whether the right chunk reaches the prompt. If it is missing, no prompt wording can save the answer."},
 {id:"p9m7q5", q:"An assistant summarizes inbound support emails, and the dashboard inserts each summary with innerHTML. In your own lab, an email with hidden instructions makes the summary include a script tag that runs for staff. What is the most reliable fix?", options:["Add 'never output HTML or scripts' to the system prompt","Switch to a larger model that is better at resisting injection","Lower the temperature so the model follows instructions exactly","Treat the output as untrusted: render it as text and validate it"], answer:3, why:"Indirect injection cannot be reliably stopped by prompt wording, because instructions and email text are all tokens to the model. Treating output as untrusted input, encoding it before it reaches HTML and validating its shape, contains the damage whatever the model writes."}
],
"p9m8":[
 {id:"p9m8q1", q:"A node holds 8 'fault' and 4 'ok'. Split A sends 4 fault left and 4 fault + 4 ok right. Split B sends 7 fault + 1 ok left and 1 fault + 3 ok right. Using weighted Gini impurity, which split does the tree pick?", options:["Split A, because it creates a perfectly pure child","Split B: weighted Gini about 0.27, versus 0.33 for A","Split A: its children average 0.25, versus about 0.30 for B","Neither: both leave more impurity than the parent's 0.44"], answer:1, why:"Parent Gini = 1 - (4/9 + 1/9) = 0.444. A: (4/12)(0) + (8/12)(0.5) = 0.333. B: (8/12)(0.219) + (4/12)(0.375) = 0.271, so B wins. Children must be weighted by size; the unweighted average and the pure child both point wrongly to A."},
 {id:"p9m8q2", q:"A random forest has 100 trees, each with variance σ² and pairwise correlation ρ = 0.5. Using ensemble variance = ρσ² + (1 - ρ)σ²/B, which change cuts the variance more?", options:["Lowering ρ to 0.2: about 0.21σ², versus 0.50σ² with 1,000 trees","Growing to 1,000 trees: the variance falls tenfold, to about 0.05σ²","Both give the same result, because B and ρ enter symmetrically","Neither, because averaging trees never reduces variance"], answer:0, why:"At 100 trees: 0.5σ² + 0.005σ² = 0.505σ². With 1,000 trees it is 0.5005σ², almost no change, because the correlated part ρσ² is a floor. With ρ = 0.2 it is 0.2σ² + 0.008σ² = 0.208σ². Feature subsampling at each split is how a forest lowers ρ."},
 {id:"p9m8q3", q:"Run k-means with k = 2 on the points 2, 4, 6, 20, 22, 24, starting from centroids 2 and 4. Where does it converge, and what is the inertia?", options:["Centroids 2 and 15.2; inertia 16","Centroids 4 and 22; inertia 8","Centroids 3 and 18; inertia 16","Centroids 4 and 22; inertia 16"], answer:3, why:"First pass: 2 goes to centroid 2 and the rest to 4, so the centroids become 2 and 15.2. Second pass: 2, 4 and 6 join the first, giving 4 and 22, and nothing changes after that. Inertia sums squared distances: (4 + 0 + 4) + (4 + 0 + 4) = 16; 8 sums plain distances."},
 {id:"p9m8q4", q:"On a dataset where 63% of rows belong to the majority class, a new model scores 66% accuracy. What is the right reading?", options:["Strong: 66% is well above a 50% coin flip on two classes","Good enough to ship if training accuracy is also 66%","Almost nothing learned: always guessing the majority scores 63%","Impossible to judge without a deep neural network to compare against"], answer:2, why:"The dummy baseline, always answering the majority class, sets the floor at 63%, so the model adds 3 points, likely within evaluation noise. Compare it with that baseline and with scaled logistic regression before trusting it. A 50% coin flip is the wrong reference for imbalanced classes."},
 {id:"p9m8q5", q:"An RBF-kernel SVM worked well on 20,000 rows. The table now has 2 million rows with missing values and categorical columns. Which is the most sensible next model?", options:["Keep the SVM and simply allow much longer training","k-NN, because it has no training cost at all","A single unlimited decision tree for maximum flexibility","Gradient-boosted trees, trained with early stopping"], answer:3, why:"SVM training grows between n^2 and n^3, so 100 times the rows means roughly 10,000 to a million times the work. k-NN moves the cost to every query, and an unlimited tree overfits. Boosted trees handle large tables and missing values and usually win there."}
],
"p9m9":[
 {id:"p9m9q1", q:"A screening model at three thresholds: 0.5 gives FN 12, FP 30; 0.3 gives FN 5, FP 90; 0.1 gives FN 2, FP 260. A miss costs 20 times a false alarm. Which threshold minimizes expected cost?", options:["0.5, because it raises the fewest false alarms","0.3, with a cost of 190 false-alarm units","0.1, because it misses the fewest cases","Whichever threshold gives the highest accuracy"], answer:1, why:"Cost = 20 x FN + FP: 0.5 gives 240 + 30 = 270; 0.3 gives 100 + 90 = 190; 0.1 gives 40 + 260 = 300, so the middle threshold wins. Minimizing only misses or only false alarms ignores the other cost, and accuracy weighs both errors equally."},
 {id:"p9m9q2", q:"A classifier scored three positives at 0.95, 0.6 and 0.35, and four negatives at 0.8, 0.5, 0.3 and 0.1. What is its AUC?", options:["About 0.67","About 0.83","0.75","0.50"], answer:2, why:"AUC is the share of positive-negative pairs in which the positive scores higher. There are 3 x 4 = 12 pairs: 0.95 beats all 4, 0.6 beats 3 and 0.35 beats 2, so 9 of 12 = 0.75. It is threshold-free and unaffected by the 3-to-4 class balance."},
 {id:"p9m9q3", q:"A code model produces n = 10 samples for a task, and c = 4 of them pass the tests. Using pass@k = 1 - C(n - c, k)/C(n, k), what are pass@1 and pass@3?", options:["pass@1 = 0.40, pass@3 = about 0.83","pass@1 = 0.40, pass@3 = 0.40","pass@1 = 0.40, pass@3 = about 0.78","pass@1 = 0.10, pass@3 = 0.30"], answer:0, why:"pass@1 = 4/10 = 0.40. pass@3 = 1 - C(6, 3)/C(10, 3) = 1 - 20/120 = 0.833, the chance that at least one of three draws from these ten passes. 0.78 is 1 - 0.6^3, which samples with replacement; the formula draws without it."},
 {id:"p9m9q4", q:"A hiring screen advances 40 of 120 applicants from group A and 20 of 90 from group B. What does the four-fifths rule of thumb indicate?", options:["Ratio 1.5 (A over B), above 0.8, so the screen passes","Ratio 0.50 (20 over 40), so the screen is flagged","The rates differ by only 0.11, so the screen passes","Ratio about 0.67, below 0.8, so it is flagged for review"], answer:3, why:"Compare rates, not counts: A advances 40/120 = 0.333 and B 20/90 = 0.222, a ratio of 0.67, below the 0.8 rule of thumb. 20/40 uses raw counts that ignore group sizes. A flag starts an investigation with domain experts; it is not proof of discrimination by itself."},
 {id:"p9m9q5", q:"A teammate wants to load a fine-tuned model, downloaded from a public hub as a pickle-based .bin file, directly on the production inference server. What is the right control?", options:["Load it directly; model files are only weights and cannot run code","Use safetensors, verify the hash, and load untrusted files in isolation","Load it once in production and watch the logs closely for errors","Rename the file's extension so the server cannot execute it"], answer:1, why:"Pickle-based model files can execute code when loaded, so a poisoned upload would run on your server. Safetensors stores tensors only; hashes or signatures and pinned versions catch tampering, and isolated loading contains anything that slips through. Renaming changes nothing about what the loader does."}
]
});
